Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Period live-action Milwaukee, late 1990s. PETER OLSEN, late twenties, slight and watchful, carries grocery bags up to a fifth-floor walk-up. His older brother NICK OLSEN, broad-shouldered, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on his chin and left eyebrow, bears six bags at once.

PETER (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about that stupid book. I’m talking about my real Big Brother.

Nick drops the bags, kisses MA OLIVIA OLSEN, sixty-five, soft-faced and frail, on the forehead.

PETER (V.O.)
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. When he walked in the bar, or the gym, people took notice.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

A MUSCLED LIFTER abandons a curl bench as Nick approaches. Nick takes the fifty-pound bar and curls it with easy contempt.

Peter watches with pride.

PETER (V.O.)
Nick is my brother. I know he’ll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK and eight-year-old PETER play kickball among dented garbage-can lids. MARK CONNER kicks the ball through MRS. ENGEL'S kitchen window.

Mrs. Engel storms out. Mark trembles.

NICK
I was the one who did it.

PETER (V.O.)
Somehow Nick gave her cash to pay for it about a week later. He never told Ma it was really Mark.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Years later. Ma sits before a game show, her pill bottles arranged in a line. Nick finds Peter in the kitchen.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don’t know. Why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill.

Peter looks toward Ma, ashamed.

NICK
You don’t need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

PETER (V.O.)
Nick says school is for nerds talking to hear themselves talk. Nick is right about most things. Or he used to be.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter wheels up on a bike for his shift. SIONNA MCALLISTER, twenty-eight, pale, slim, short brown hair, light-blue eyes, black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a fish-symbol necklace, trips on the cement and brushes against him.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different. I never heard of that.

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

Peter cannot stop looking into her eyes.

PETER
Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on Peter’s receipt.

PETER (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. She was intense.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma searches the kitchen counter in a panic.

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, your pills are right in front of you.

Ma stares at Peter with glassy, empty eyes. Then she sees the bottles.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don’t know what got into me.

Peter watches her, frightened.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Packers football plays over the bar. Nick slaps Peter on the back.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
Ma was confused today. She didn’t know where her pills were.

Nick’s face reddens.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

PETER
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK
Do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

Peter puts down his beer.

PETER (V.O.)
God, sometimes I really hate Nick inside.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter enters the grand old library, snow visible through tall windows. He approaches LIBRARIAN ELAINE, fifty, neat gray hair, gentle eyes.

PETER
How do I take a book home from here?

ELAINE
Do you have a library card?

PETER
No. How do I get one of those?

Elaine gives him a form. Later, Peter walks the classic literature shelves and takes down Wuthering Heights.

PETER (V.O.)
Who needed school anyway when you could get the same thing for free in the library?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Students fill the coffee shop. Sionna sits at a back table in a loose green hooded top, her backpack at her feet. Peter arrives clutching Wuthering Heights.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
Yea, I know. I read it.

Peter winces. Sionna smiles.

SIONNA
The environment of the book is like its people. Wild and unpredictable and infertile. Heathcliff’s love for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

PETER
A love that never changes will destroy you.

SIONNA
Sometimes.

PETER (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake. It was like I could talk to Sionna about anything and she wouldn’t think it was stupid.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter wakes from a dream, sweating. Sionna sits with him on her worn brown couch, calm and attentive.

PETER
I was on a horse with you. You had old clothes on. We stopped in a field and I kissed you.

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

PETER
If that was a past life, I wish I could go back there.

SIONNA
Some people think dreaming is real, and being awake is not.

PETER
I’m sitting in a chair. I’m breathing air. I’m looking at you. How could all this be fake?

SIONNA
It’s not fake. It’s just not something that will last for more than the present moment we are experiencing it.

Peter absorbs this.

SIONNA
We have to be prepared to change with it. To let go of things, and accept things, without grasping onto them.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Peter, in a T-shirt and shorts, struggles through a crowded yoga class. His shirt falls over his face in downward dog. Sionna suppresses a laugh.

Later, everyone lies still on purple mats.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

PETER
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

They smile.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Nick stands over Peter, fists clenching. Ma watches from her chair, confused.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? With who?

PETER
I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That’s all.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

Nick storms out. The apartment door slams.

PETER (V.O.)
Ma needs me. Nick needs me because he needs me to take care of Ma. Sionna needs me.

Peter stops himself.

PETER (V.O.)
Sionna needs me? Maybe I was just hoping.

INT. DOCTOR CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

Ma clutches her chest, dizzy. PETER sits opposite DR. CARSON, sixty, grave and measured.

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke.

PETER
She’s been takin’ her pills. I make sure she takes them religiously.

DR. CARSON
Take her directly to the emergency room. She needs observation.

Peter reaches for the phone.

PETER
I have to call Nick.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma sleeps in a hospital bed. Peter dozes in a chair.

A DREAM: A muddy battlefield. Peter, in an old red tunic, stands over a dying soldier.

DYING SOLDIER
Save her.

Nick appears through smoke, armed with a long knife.

PETER
Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK
That you are, little brother.

Nick stabs Peter in the chest.

BACK TO SCENE: Peter wakes gasping. Ma stirs in bed.

PETER (V.O.)
Why would Nick want to kill me? His little brother.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma has returned home. A blue weekly pill organizer sits on the table. Sionna helps Peter explain it to Ma.

PETER
Right here is every day of the week. Morning and night.

MA
You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me.

Nick enters in work boots and a brown suede jacket. He sees Sionna.

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you.

NICK
Uh, yea. Likewise.

Sionna offers him peach pie. Nick refuses.

NICK
Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

SIONNA
All right. Don’t want to make you feel left out.

Nick glares.

NICK
So, I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
That’s right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I have a major in English and a minor in Philosophy. I’m thinking college professor.

NICK
It’s tough out there. It’s even harder for a woman to teach at that level.

Sionna sets down her fork.

SIONNA
There are more females in college today than males. Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

Nick is silenced. Peter tries not to smile.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Nick drinks with BOB and SAMMY. Lindsey stands near the pool tables with her new boyfriend, CARLOS.

NICK
Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

PETER
Why does it matter, Bro? You broke up with her.

Nick advances toward Carlos.

CARLOS
You got a problem with me?

NICK
Hell, I never even seen you before, and you’re in my face like a little bitch.

Peter steps between them.

PETER
Nick, listen. You don’t even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK
Keep out of it!

PETER
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick swings. His fist catches Peter across the face.

Joe and BOUNCER HARRIS rush in to restrain Nick. Peter stumbles back, stunned, then runs from the bar.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Peter runs through the cold until he cannot breathe.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter collapses into Sionna’s arms. She cups his bruised cheek.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

PETER
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
No one does that, you know. No one who really cares about you.

PETER
I love Nick. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You can’t let him intimidate you, or stop you from your purpose.

PETER
My purpose. What is my purpose?

SIONNA
The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you’re here.

Peter rests his head in her lap.

PETER
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

Months later. Peter, now carrying college books, finds the apartment empty. Ma’s coat and purse are gone. The answering machine blinks.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, graying and solemn, faces Peter across a desk.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Last night, about eight p.m., your brother and your mother were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe.

Peter goes still.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital. Your mother passed away at the scene.

PETER
No.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology is pending. Your brother may have been drunk.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter sits in Ma’s empty chair, clutching her afghan. Sionna enters and kneels beside him.

PETER
She wouldn’t be sitting there anymore. She’s dead.

Peter breaks apart.

PETER
And Nick killed her.

Sionna holds him while he sobs.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick lies battered beneath tubes, his right leg immobilized. Peter stands at the bedside, rigid with rage.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man. Can’t believe Ma’s gone.

PETER
The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK
They’re telling me it could be vehicular manslaughter. I shouldn’t have to deal with this. It was a mistake.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick’s anger flares, but he cannot rise from the bed.

NICK
You think I meant to kill her?

PETER
Think about it, Nick.

Peter turns and leaves.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Nick pounds on the front door. Sionna opens it a crack. He forces his way inside.

NICK
You’re the reason I’m probably going to jail.

SIONNA
What makes you think I’m responsible?

NICK
If it wasn’t for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. He would be taking care of Ma. She would be alive right now.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down.

Nick traps her against the hallway wall and draws a switchblade.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight or else.

The front door bursts open. Peter enters.

PETER
Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

Peter grabs Nick’s arm. The blade jerks into Sionna’s upper chest. Blood blooms across her shirt.

Sionna collapses. Nick shoves Peter away and flees.

PETER
Sionna! Sionna!

Peter presses both hands against the wound.

PETER
Stay with me. Please stay with me.

SIRENS rise outside.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

Late fall. Peter places golden mums beside Ma’s grave.

The marker reads: MOTHER: OLIVIA ANNE OLSEN.

PETER (V.O.)
I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.

Peter looks out over the cemetery.

PETER
Yes, you did, Ma.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - DAY

Sionna, her left shoulder braced and her light-blue eyes emptied of their old spark, sits in a rocking chair. Peter crouches before her.

PETER
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
You say that, but deep inside I still have my doubts.

Peter’s face crumples.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. Maybe I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

PETER
Stay here with me. We can work things out.

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

She walks slowly into the bedroom. Peter remains alone.

PETER (V.O.)
She was my life guide. I relied on her to fill up my consciousness.

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DAY

Eight years later. PETER, more confident, dressed in a dark coat, walks through Pike Place Market. Seattle rain mists the pavement.

PETER (V.O.)
I was two people: the child in Wisconsin and now the man in Washington.

A sign for TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA hangs beneath a medical office building.

PETER (V.O.)
Thanks to Tony, I had done pretty well for myself. I had my own chain of pizzerias in the Northwest.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Peter lies in meditation among candles. The instructor’s voice is soft.

INSTRUCTOR MAYA
Think of things you’re grateful for.

Peter closes his eyes.

PETER (V.O.)
Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison.

A DREAM: Peter walks a bright green road beside a SAGE in white flowing cloth. A dirty pizza box lies in the grass.

PETER
It’s terrible. It’s an eyesore.

SAGE
Stop judging things. Things are neither good nor bad. This is an illusion.

The Sage and pizza box vanish. Peter smiles in his sleep.

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter wakes beside BOZO, his orange-and-white cat. He opens his laptop and books a flight.

PETER
I have to find forgiveness.

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

Peter stands before the old brick building. He looks up at the fifth-floor windows, then toward the alley.

PETER (V.O.)
I had accepted the role of Nick’s serf. His lowly servant.

Peter breathes out and turns away.

EXT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION - DAY

Peter crosses a gravel lot toward the severe brick prison.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTION VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass divides Peter from Nick. Nick is thinner now, gray at the temples, his once-fierce face collapsed into sorrow.

They lift their phones.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

PETER
Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally.

NICK
It’s looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe as early as two more years.

PETER
Good for you, Nick.

Peter grips the receiver.

PETER
I guess we don’t have a ton of time, so I’m here to tell you some things. I would appreciate it if you would just listen.

NICK
Yeah. Okay, man.

PETER
Even though you are my brother, I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me.

Nick bows his head.

PETER
You’re an asshole that killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my entire life, forcing me to take care of Ma so you could have the more exciting life.

Peter’s voice breaks, but he continues.

PETER
Mostly, you’re an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life, which drove her away.

Nick’s eyes fill.

PETER
But this is the most important bit. I forgive you.

Nick looks up.

PETER
I forgive you for being a huge asshole because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears slide down Nick’s face.

NICK
I’m so sorry. You’re so right, man. I am a huge asshole.

PETER
I want you to get better. I still believe you can change.

NICK
What are you doing now?

PETER
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’ll be expanding.

Nick smiles through tears.

NICK
President Olsen. That’s fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man.

An OFFICER approaches.

OFFICER HARRIS
Time’s up.

PETER
After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too.

Peter hangs up. Nick is led away down the hall.

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

Peter walks toward the terminal with a small bag over his shoulder. He pauses, looks toward the open sky, and smiles.

PETER (V.O.)
The weight of thirty years was gone. I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment.

Peter checks his boarding pass.

PETER
That’s me. President Peter Olsen.

FADE OUT.
